  rather than designing them yet.

  The next fork is identity, and it's the one that most shapes both the backend choice and how much work A is.

  A team app needs to know who did something — the change log is worthless if every entry says "someone changed this."
  But "know who" spans a wide range. At the light end, people pick their name from a list on first visit and it's stored
  locally; there's no password, anyone with the URL can claim to be anyone. That's genuinely fine for four trusted
  colleagues on an internal tool, and it removes auth entirely from A. At the heavy end you have real accounts with
  email verification, sessions, and password reset — which is a meaningful chunk of work that has nothing to do with
  tasks or notifications, and is the single most common way a project like this stalls before reaching the interesting
  part.

  The middle option, magic links, is the one I'd reach for: you get real identity tied to a real email address, no
  password storage, no reset flow — and email addresses are exactly what sub-project D needs later anyway.

  What it costs you to go light now: upgrading later means backfilling identity onto existing change-log entries and
  task rows. That's annoying but bounded, as long as A stores a stable user id rather than a display name on every
  record. I'd design for that regardless.
